==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=intimacy (disambiguation)|intimacy}}

===Etymology===
{{suf|en|intimate|cy}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪn.tɪ.mə.si/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|countable}} [[feeling|Feeling]] or [[atmosphere]] of [[closeness]] and [[openness]] towards someone else, not necessarily involving [[sexuality]].
#* '''1792''', {{w|Mary Wollstonecraft}}, ''{{w|A Vindication of the Rights of Woman}}'', Section 13.6<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3420/pg3420-images.html]</sup>
#*: To adulterous lust the most sacred duties are sacrificed, because, before marriage, men, by a promiscuous '''intimacy''' with women, learned to consider love as a selfish gratification—learned to separate it not only from esteem, but from the affection merely built on habit, which mixes a little humanity with it.
#* '''1879''', {{w|Robert Louis Stevenson}}, “Truth of Intercourse” in ''Essays, English and American'', The Harvard Classics, Volume 28, edited by Charles W. Eliot, New York: P.F. Collier & Son, 1910, p. 287,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/essaysenglishan01lowegoog]</sup>
#*: The habitual liar may be a very honest fellow, and live truly with his wife and friends; while another man who never told a formal falsehood in his life may yet be himself one lie—heart and face, from top to bottom. This is the kind of lie which poisons '''intimacy'''.
#* '''1908''', {{w|Jack London}}, “{{w|To Build a Fire}}” in ''{{w|Lost Face}}'', London: Mills & Boon, 1916,<sup>[https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2429/2429-h/2429-h.htm]</sup>
#*: {{...}} there was keen '''intimacy''' between the dog and the man.
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=May 27, 2012
|author=Nathan Rabin
|title=TV: Review: THE SIMPSONS (CLASSIC): “New Kid On The Block” (season 4, episode 8; originally aired 11/12/1992)
|work=The Onion AV Club
|url=http://www.avclub.com/articles/new-kid-on-the-block,75341/
|page=
|passage=The Conan O’Brien-penned half-hour has the capacity to rip our collective hearts out the way the cute, funny bad girl next door does to Bart when she reveals that her new boyfriend is Jimbo Jones, but the show keeps shying away from genuine emotion in favor of jokes that, while overwhelmingly funny, detract from the poignancy and the emotional '''intimacy''' of the episode.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} Intimate [[relationship]].
#* '''1787''', {{w|Robert Burns}}, Letter to Dr. Moore, 23 April, 1787, in J. Logie Robertson (ed.), ''The Letters of Robert Burns, Selected and Arranged, with an Introduction'', London: Walter Scott, 1887, p. 57,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/lettersselecteda00burnuoft]</sup>
#*: I have formed many '''intimacies''' and friendships here, but I am afraid they are all of too tender a construction to bear carriage a hundred and fifty miles.
#* '''1815''', {{w|Jane Austen}}, ''[[w:Emma (novel)|Emma]]'', Volume I, Chapter 8,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/158/158-h/158-h.htm]</sup>
#*: “I have always thought it a very foolish '''intimacy''',” said Mr. Knightley presently, “though I have kept my thoughts to myself; but I now perceive that it will be a very unfortunate one for Harriet {{...}}”
#* '''1899''', {{w|Henry James}}, ''{{w|The Awkward Age}}'', Book One, Chapter 2,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/7433/old/akage10.txt]</sup>
#*: “{{...}} it isn’t my notion of the way to bring up a girl to give her up, in extreme youth, to an '''intimacy''' with a young married woman who’s both unhappy and silly, whose conversation has absolutely no limits, who says everything that comes into her head and talks to the poor child about God only knows what {{...}}”
# {{lb|en|countable|especially plural}} Intimate [[detail]], ([[item]] of) intimate [[information]].
#* '''1961''', {{w|V. S. Naipaul}}, ''{{w|A House for Mr Biswas}}'', Vintage International, 2001, Part One, Chapter 4,
#*: He recognized the tone as the one used by friendly sisters to discuss the infirmities of their husbands. It was Shama’s plea to a sister to exchange '''intimacies''', to show support.

